  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1219 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nSaturday is shaping up to be the warmest day of the extended \nforecast before the pattern shifts colder for the rest of the \nweekend and into next week. An upper low swings into the Great \nPlains and cold front is pushed across the region early Sunday \nushering in cooler and drier air. Lows fall into the upper 30s to \n40s for most with some of the colder locations (higher elevations, \nnorthern Lea County) dipping near the freezing mark. Highs in the \n50s to 60s hold on through Tuesday before a backdoor front pushes \ninto the area. This looks to knock down daytime temperatures a \ncouple degrees more with the Permian Basin struggling to reach 50F \nduring the afternoon. \n\nFor Thanksgiving, the pattern will still be on the cooler side with \nhighs near 50F and lows in the 30s and 40s for much of the region. \nToo much uncertainty remains to give substantive answers as far \nprecipitation is concerned. For now, the forecast will remain dry \ndespite a couple ensemble solutions pointing to some low (10-20%) \nchances for precipitation on or just after Thanksgiving. Stay tuned \nfor updates!   \n\n-Stickney",
